  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather prevails for the rest of this week\nwith warm above normal temperatures. Next rain chance comes \nthis weekend from a potential tropical system.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to slide east into the Atlantic\ntoday and flow becomes more west-southwest oriented. This will\ncontinue into the end of the week, resulting in above normal \ntemperatures into the mid/upper 70s and some lower 80s. Along\nwith warm above normal temperatures, dry and mostly clear\nconditions will also persist through Friday.\n\nAttention then turns to the Gulf over the weekend with model\nguidance now coming into better agreement of a tropical system\nmaking landfall along the Gulf Coast and then moving north\nthrough the lower Mississippi Valley region and ultimately \nthrough the Ohio Valley by Sunday. Confidence continues to\nincrease that a period of areawide rain will fall beginning\nSaturday, continuing into Sunday. Overall, rainfall appears to \nbe beneficial given forecast amounts of 1 to 2 inches falling \nover a 48 hour period...therefore flooding threat appears to be \nlow at this time. However, favorable southeast flow, given the \ncurrent forecast track, may allow for localized higher amounts \nalong the eastern slopes of the Blue Ridge.\n\nRain appears to be the greatest threat, but some gusty southeast\ndownsloping winds possible along favored western slopes from the\nMountain Empire of Virginia northward into southeast West \nVirginia. There is the potential for gusts ranging from 25 -\n35mph late Saturday night through Sunday.\n\nOverall, with this system still not developed yet, this portion\nof the forecast for the late weekend still remains uncertain \nand low confidence at this time.",
